WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.960
I made a comment in July that EDH has effectively&nbsp;
become the casual way to play paper Magic. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.560
it really has—the days of 60 card kitchen table&nbsp;
gaming are in the past, replaced by a diverging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.680
path for newer players: either play Arena and&nbsp;
get your 1v1 fix from the comfort of your couch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.560
or meet up with three other people and play&nbsp;
commander. The nostalgic part of me certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.920
wants to push back against this, and I’ve spoken&nbsp;
before about some reasons why commander perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.120
isn’t the best on-ramp for newer players. But,&nbsp;
there are some clear upsides to commander—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.000
fact that it’s a casual format by designation&nbsp;
rather than being casual gameplay on the fringes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.200
of competitive formats means a much greater&nbsp;
degree of stability than something like 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.440
card kitchen table magic, a format whose current&nbsp;
form basically vanishes off the face of the earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.320
whenever the friends who want to play it with&nbsp;
you stop wanting to play it. More importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.680
commander as a format clearly isn’t&nbsp;
going anywhere in the casual scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.720
so it’s pointless to try and fight against it.&nbsp;
That said, the collapse of 60 card casual Magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.080
along with the slow decline in the popularity of&nbsp;
draft that I’ve noticed over the last ten years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.640
have had me thinking—what are we losing, exactly?&nbsp;
What impact is it having that there is a growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.680
population of commander players who have played&nbsp;
little-to-none of other formats? To be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:15.200
this isn’t intended as old man grumbling—I&nbsp;
can certainly get old man grumbly sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.680
as somebody who has been playing Magic for&nbsp;
more than half of my life, and maybe I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.520
be more old man grumbly if I were a Modern player&nbsp;
or something. But no, this is a genuine question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.200
and to get some insight into it, I’m going to&nbsp;
answer a slightly different set of questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.280
in this video: what does the experience of playing&nbsp;
formats that aren’t commander endow upon a player?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.080
What impact might that sort of experience have&nbsp;
on the way they play commander? And, do these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.240
impacts make it worth venturing outside EDH&nbsp;
from time to time for us primarily EDH players?

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.240
To start with, let’s talk about the gameplay&nbsp;
experience of a 20 health 1v1 format. A defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.120
feature of these formats as compared to commander&nbsp;
is that they’re low slung and grindy, with smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.520
threats being much more relevant. With this fact&nbsp;
in mind, what I’m about to say may initially seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.160
a bit confusing, that being that playing these&nbsp;
formats can directly help with building the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.920
skills required for playing commander. Commander&nbsp;
and 20 health formats certainly are different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.320
but counterintuitively this is exactly why it&nbsp;
can be helpful to have played the latter—it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.560
give a player better breadth of experience.&nbsp;
See, playing commander gives players lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.640
experience in fighting against big, bombastic&nbsp;
wincons—for starters you’ve got the obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.800
examples like combo decks and combo-adjacent&nbsp;
plays like a massive X spell or a Craterhoof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.960
on a board full of tokens, plays that reduce one&nbsp;
or more life totals to zero in one fell swoop and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.280
often do so with large excesses of damage.&nbsp;
However, even outside of these, life total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.640
can frequently feel irrelevant in EDH, regardless&nbsp;
of whether it is or not. Even with a conventional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.240
big creatures midrange deck without a splashy&nbsp;
singular finisher, a lot of times most damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.000
will be dealt in one or two attack swings. This&nbsp;
comes down to the snowbally nature of a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.920
EDH decks—all sorts of cards are intended to push&nbsp;
a deck to greater and greater volumes of gameplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.840
whether that be effect doublers or mana doublers&nbsp;
or other forms of action-compounding engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.960
This often means that decks buckle down until&nbsp;
they have a massive board of creatures to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.160
attack with before swinging wide, sometimes&nbsp;
for entire life totals in one go. Somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.520
might have to count out 35 damage to this&nbsp;
player, 29 damage to that player, and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.600
and under that context opponents don’t even&nbsp;
really need to think about their life total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.040
regardless of if a handful of points of difference&nbsp;
would have been relevant or not. Like c’mon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.640
you lost because the opponent popped off, simple&nbsp;
as. However, what this means is that commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.200
players are often less prepared for grindier&nbsp;
games where every point of life total matters.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.400
The results of this can be seen when it comes to&nbsp;
blocking and life total management—players who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.720
are more experienced in formats where life totals&nbsp;
are relevant will be much more careful of certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.040
life total thresholds, and more willing to block&nbsp;
to stay above them. Players of 20 health formats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.840
learn to internalize the idea that life total is&nbsp;
a resource, and to know when to spend and when to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.760
save that resource, but commander players&nbsp;
who have spent a lot of time dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:00.000
bigger one-or-two-shot wincons won’t internalize&nbsp;
this lesson nearly as quickly. EDH’s higher life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.160
totals also train players to mostly disregard&nbsp;
the idea of blocking with useful creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.200
because it feels better to keep them around and&nbsp;
most of the time that’s the correct decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.360
in strategy terms as well. Unlike in a 20 health&nbsp;
format where blocking with a key creature to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.680
alive for an extra turn or two is a very common&nbsp;
necessity that players get used to biting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:24.240
bullet on, this isn’t necessary nearly as often in&nbsp;
EDH due to higher life totals and heftier wincons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.280
and this means that when it becomes a necessity&nbsp;
a lot of players instinctually refuse to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.960
even when it means sacrificing an amount&nbsp;
of life that will almost certainly lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.840
them a game. Another aspect of non-commander&nbsp;
gameplay, and particularly limited formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.840
is low-resource environments where players are&nbsp;
topdecking or working under tight constraints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.280
in some other way. These games aren’t wildly&nbsp;
common in commander due to how common ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:52.880
and draw are—with variation by playgroup,&nbsp;
obviously—and this means that you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.280
play a lot more EDH to get comfortable under these&nbsp;
circumstances than you would to get comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.280
under more common EDH scenarios. The results&nbsp;
of this can be quite problematic—when a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.800
has involved some form of stax or heavy&nbsp;
attrition and resources are dwindling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.440
you can usually tell who has played other&nbsp;
formats and who hasn’t. A lot of primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.880
EDH players will be looking at the situation as&nbsp;
a lost cause, a game gone wildly off the rails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.760
whereas people who have done their time in the&nbsp;
trenches of limited or 60 card formats will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.200
be more engaged and vigilant, looking for outs&nbsp;
and bits of incremental advantage to grab onto.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.840
Outside of gameplay, the experience of building&nbsp;
60 card decks can help with EDH deckbuilding as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.200
well. One way this can manifest is when building&nbsp;
decks which are exceptions to traditional norms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.920
EDH deckbuilding. See, people tend to have rough&nbsp;
templates they follow when they build commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.040
decks, myself included—a low double digits&nbsp;
number of draw spells is good for maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.920
a consistent stream of cards turn over turn, maybe&nbsp;
more if those draw spells are smaller or less if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.520
the deck has other ways to compound value or&nbsp;
tutors to find its value engines. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.680
a low double digits amount of ramp will&nbsp;
be good for a deck that wants to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.520
single piece of ramp most games but is fine&nbsp;
occasionally not having one, and adjustments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.200
can be made up or down depending on how often a&nbsp;
deck wants ramp, what size of ramp a deck wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.200
and how aggressively a player is willing&nbsp;
to mulligan. These are references I follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.080
and I like them as a starting point because&nbsp;
they’re flexible—they prescribe numbers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.040
cards based on what a deck wants to do and what&nbsp;
other tools a deck has available to it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.880
the trouble comes when references like these are&nbsp;
less flexible, and when they’re treated as gospel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.520
regardless of what deck they’re being applied to.&nbsp;
Ramp is a great example for demonstrating this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.200
because it’s one of the biggest differences&nbsp;
between EDH and other formats. Basically all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.920
EDH decks have some amount of ramp in them, and&nbsp;
most have a decent amount—8-10 slots spent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.320
ramp is common. By contrast, most 60 card decks&nbsp;
do not—you will get specific decks focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.480
high-cost plays, such as Tron, that focus&nbsp;
a lot of deck space on ramping, and you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.840
get midrange decks that run a spattering of&nbsp;
efficient dorks to hit their impactful 3, 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.680
and 5 drops a turn early in some games to&nbsp;
gain a tempo advantage. Broadly, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.480
ramp isn’t widely played in 60 card formats&nbsp;
because it often isn’t worthwhile to expend a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.600
just for a bit of acceleration. This is likewise&nbsp;
true in limited—I’ll run an efficient dork like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.840
a Llanowar Elves and I’ll run a card like Dance&nbsp;
of the Tumbleweeds or Poison Dart Frog that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.920
some extra utility attached at a good rate, and&nbsp;
occasionally I’ll add some lesser ramp if I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.520
critical high-cost plays that my gameplan ended&nbsp;
up needing to revolve around. Aside from those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.680
instances, though, it’s almost never correct&nbsp;
to play ramp in draft or sealed—draw is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.200
sparse, and mana and cards are better spent&nbsp;
contesting the board and threatening life totals.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.400
The result of all of this is that in formats&nbsp;
outside commander, ramp spells are viewed very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:39.040
critically—a player must ask themself “why is this&nbsp;
here?” and if that question doesn’t have a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.360
and persuasive answer, the card will generally get&nbsp;
cut. By contrast, many people building a commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:49.360
deck will throw together their template-built EDH&nbsp;
deck with the requisite count of ramp cards picked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.400
from among the available staples. These will&nbsp;
often be added without consideration for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.200
the cards are accomplishing—you’ll get Cultivate&nbsp;
in two-color decks with a four mana commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:02.920
Gilded Lotus in decks with an average mana value&nbsp;
of 2.5, Rampant Growth in an artifact-centered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:07.240
deck with a three mana commander, and so on.&nbsp;
Fundamentally, the issue is that a lot of players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:11.840
don’t stop to consider what ramp is offering them,&nbsp;
because they’re playing a format where it is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.680
unquestionable fact of life—I play ramp because&nbsp;
I want more mana, c’mon. Now, in most decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.920
this won’t be the end of the world—decks do want&nbsp;
ramp spells, and even if players add ramp that’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:27.000
bit suboptimal for their deck, their deck is still&nbsp;
spending slots on a thing that it needs, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.000
specifics can be corrected later. However, just&nbsp;
as there’s variety in how much ramp 60 card decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.560
want there will be some commander decks that&nbsp;
don’t really want ramp in any sort of conventional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.200
quantities. I have two different decks where&nbsp;
this is the case—in both my Malfegor deck and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:46.040
Francisco Malcolm deck I don’t want to spend the&nbsp;
entirety of my turn 2 or 3 playing ramp spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.440
because I have more useful cards I want to be&nbsp;
developing. Both decks include a handful of rocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:54.840
for purposes of weaving into a later turn, because&nbsp;
this is still EDH we’re talking about and getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.680
a future mana boost off of a 1 mana investment&nbsp;
is still useful for keeping up in later turns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:04.240
but these aren’t a key card type and each deck&nbsp;
runs only a low single digits quantity of two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.960
mana rocks so as not to draw more than one in the&nbsp;
early stages of a game, plus a Sol Ring to cache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.040
in on my Wizard of the Coast mandated dose of&nbsp;
variance. The step of asking how much mana a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.280
actually needs and what times it will want to play&nbsp;
ramp spells is one that a lot of players forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:23.320
to complete, and playing other formats where the&nbsp;
inclusion of ramp isn’t such a foregone conclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:27.680
is great for getting perspective on what these&nbsp;
cards are actually accomplishing in a deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.520
The same can go for other card types—when is it&nbsp;
correct to exclude or go light on card draw? When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:37.120
is it correct to exclude board wipes? These are&nbsp;
things that EDH players will consider necessities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.360
but depending on the deck they might not be&nbsp;
nearly as important as a player might assume.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.920
One other deckbuilding skill that EDH isn’t great&nbsp;
at teaching players is figuring out optimal card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.640
quantities. A feature of singleton formats is that&nbsp;
they restrict how many of a particular sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.480
card a player can run. Some cards will have many&nbsp;
similar options, but other types of cards will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:59.520
be more unique, and a player will have no choice&nbsp;
but to add the number of it that exist to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.560
deck and no more, regardless of how much they&nbsp;
like it. This outsources a lot of decisionmaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.480
to the nature of the format—players don’t get&nbsp;
to choose how many cards to add to their deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.720
that create additional dragons when a dragon&nbsp;
attacks, that decision is already made for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.240
them. Whereas in 60 card formats up to 4 of a&nbsp;
given card can be run, giving players room to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:22.160
flex counts on various cards up and down and&nbsp;
see how they feel, in EDH there often isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.360
this same level of choice. The result of this&nbsp;
is that when players have access to a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:31.240
number of a particularly appealing effect,&nbsp;
if they’ve played EDH but not other formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:35.880
the instinct might be to load up on all available&nbsp;
copies of that effect, regardless of how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.440
would actually be optimal for the deck. This is&nbsp;
likely something that will be figured out in due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:44.080
time so long as players are willing to challenge&nbsp;
their assumptions about how their decks work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:49.040
from time to time, but having less of an intuitive&nbsp;
sense for how many copies of a card is appropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.960
is certainly something that can lead to wasted&nbsp;
time and games played with an off-kilter deck.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:59.240
Long story short, yes, playing formats outside&nbsp;
EDH can imbue players with important experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:04.280
experiences that will be especially useful&nbsp;
on the fringes of the commander experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:08.760
Commander players will have no trouble getting&nbsp;
used to playing against snowbally value piles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:13.160
dealing with board wipes, building conventional&nbsp;
synergy-based midrange, developing a growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:18.000
knowledge of common staples, and so on. Playing&nbsp;
lots of commander will make you better at a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.360
things in commander, and playing enough commander&nbsp;
in diverse environments will even grant a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:26.200
of proficiency in the things I mentioned&nbsp;
earlier—life total management, low resource&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:31.640
environments, unconventional deck types, and so&nbsp;
on. These aren’t forbidden knowledge or anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:35.680
but playing formats outside EDH will certainly&nbsp;
help to instill certain lessons faster than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:41.040
others, and develop an intuitive sense for why&nbsp;
they’re true. It’s also good to play other formats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.720
because cards are designed for other formats,&nbsp;
and the further back in time a card was printed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.560
the more it was focused on 1v1 formats. As much as&nbsp;
it can feel like us commander players are playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:54.960
in our own little bubble, we’re playing with&nbsp;
cards that are part of a broader game, and having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.240
a better understanding of all facets of that&nbsp;
game will help you as a player across the board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.720
If nothing else, it’s good to be aware of the&nbsp;
areas of play that we might be a bit weaker in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:06.000
and try to gain a bit of experience in them.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.800
To wrap this up, what formats should you&nbsp;
be playing? As far as 60 card formats go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.680
I can’t speak for the quality of most of them&nbsp;
because I simply haven’t played them recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:20.240
with one exception—Pauper. This all-commons&nbsp;
format is cheap to get into and pretty vibrant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:25.560
and its fast-paced but not overly complex gameplay&nbsp;
makes it a great place to dive into 60 card Magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.840
Switching gears to limited, my personal favorite&nbsp;
way to play Magic outside of EDH is draft and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:34.360
cube—the process of drafting from packs and&nbsp;
deckbuilding with the resulting pool is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.840
skill-testing task all on its own that will appeal&nbsp;
to anybody who loves deckbuilding and also has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:43.840
bit of a competitive or spike-adjacent streak.&nbsp;
If those two options sound too intimidating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:48.080
if nothing else you should go to prereleases.&nbsp;
These are sealed events—you open six packs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:51.360
and build a deck with them, simple as.&nbsp;
I personally prefer draft to sealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:56.280
but the exact reasons that make a longtime player&nbsp;
like me have this preference make sealed a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:00.320
option for players without as much experience&nbsp;
playing limited. Deckbuilding is more streamlined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.400
and less punishing of mistakes since decks can&nbsp;
be totally rebuilt between rounds if it becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.800
clear something went wrong, and variance plays a&nbsp;
greater role in success which means that a less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:12.600
experienced player who pulls the right selection&nbsp;
of cards can have a fighting chance even against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:17.160
a Magic veteran. If you want to hear more about&nbsp;
why it’s worth playing non-commander formats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.400
what formats you should get into, there’s&nbsp;
a whole episode that just released on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:25.120
podcast that I co-host goes into more depth about&nbsp;
this, and a link to that is in the description.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:29.240
With that said, thanks for watching! And thanks&nbsp;
to Archidekt for supporting the channel. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.400
my favorite parts about the site is the&nbsp;
visually streamlined folder system that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:36.520
lets me keep my decks organized with ease. A link&nbsp;
to my Archidekt profile is in the description if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:40.200
you want to browse the various folders there&nbsp;
and the decks contained within. As always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.920
I also want to thank my Patrons, some of whom&nbsp;
are on screen now. My final deck review for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.080
the year is taking submissions now, so if&nbsp;
you want a chance at getting selected for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:51.280
that you can sign up for my Patreon for $3 a&nbsp;
month. You’d also be receiving access to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.960
back catalogue of bonus videos, which span back&nbsp;
